design:
# Guest SDK: option (a) sketch

Status: sketch, not an approved design. Bead 1 of this epic turns it into the design.

One Rust core, compiled to a WebAssembly component and wrapped per language. The rules live once in `morphir-workspace` (`discover_with_identity`, `SourceIdentity` in `crates/morphir-workspace/src/discovery/identity.rs`). The component exports `discover` and imports `source-identity`, which each language implements with its module-naming rule.

```mermaid
flowchart TB
  core["morphir-workspace: discover_with_identity"] --> guest["morphir-workspace-guest (wit-bindgen)"]
  guest -->|"wasm32-wasip2"| wasm["workspace-discovery.wasm (released with checksums)"]
  wasm --> ts["@finos/morphir-extension-sdk (jco)"]
  wasm --> py["morphir-extension-sdk (wasmtime-py)"]
  elm["morphir-elm vnext: ElmIdentity"] --> ts
  corpus["shared corpus"] -.-> core
  corpus -.-> ts
  corpus -.-> py
```

```wit
package morphir:workspace@0.1.0;

interface source-identity {
  record diagnostic { code: string, message: string }
  module-name: func(root: string, path: string, text: string) -> result<string, diagnostic>;
}

world discovery {
  import source-identity;
  export discover: func(request: discovery-request) -> discovery-response;
}
```

Rust guests keep calling `discover_with_identity` directly. The TypeScript package instantiates the jco output with the language's `SourceIdentity`; the Elm extension's `cli2/mep/workspace.ts` shrinks to its Elm naming rule plus one `discover` call. Python uses wasmtime-py component bindings the same way.

Kept honest by: one shared corpus run by the core, the component and each package; the component released as a morphir-rust artifact and pinned by the packages; request and response types generated from the MEP model once the discovery payloads are in it (morphir-opua.1.2.2, morphir-opua.1.2.4).

Trade-off accepted: packages depend on component tooling (wit-bindgen, jco, wasmtime-py), shared with the Phase D host wrappers (morphir-opua.1.4.3, morphir-opua.1.4.4). Bead 4 proves the component on the corpus before the packages build on it.

Open questions for bead 1: where the corpus lives (spec/mep/mck or a new spec/workspace) and whether the discovery payloads join the MEP Gleam model; package names; whether the Scala copy in morphir-scala-elm is in scope; whether the epic also covers the rest of the guest API in TypeScript and Python (lifecycle, describe, claims, compile envelope).

